flsplash -- the FreeLFS boot screen

Two styles, chosen system-wide in /etc/freelfs/splash.conf (splash=flat or
splash=legacy; a missing or unreadable file, or anything else, means flat):

  flat (default)  the logo of docs/logo.svg as a flat F in the accent green on
                  the background #0d1110, a thin spinning arc under it (a 110
                  degree arc, one turn per 1.2 s, antialiased, constant speed),
                  and "FreeLFS" small at the bottom in the light text colour.
                  Fades in from black in 500 ms, keeps spinning until the
                  handover, then fades to the background (300 ms) and lets go.
  legacy (3D)     the glossy logo: beveled green slabs, a soft glow, a faded
                  mirror image, a light band that sweeps across, and "FreeLFS"
                  under it. About 3.8 s, then fades out and lets go.

Both start on the same clock. The minimum on screen is 3 s from the first picture
(flat: it is never ended before that; legacy: its animation is 3.8 s).

How: nothing is drawn at boot, only composited. genart.c renders the layers
once at build time (bevel normals, diffuse and specular light, a fake studio
environment, the highlight, 6 x 6 or 4 x 4 supersampling) in six sizes,
deflated into flsplash.dat, which is built into the binary (about 0.5 MB).
At boot the size that fits the screen height is picked (up to 40% of it), so
nothing is ever scaled: no blur. The word is part of the art and never drawn
again, so the last frame is exactly what stays on screen until the login
screen takes over. Only the logo region is composited per frame, in
premultiplied integer math, two channels per 32 bit word. The clock is
CLOCK_MONOTONIC from the first picture: a slow machine drops frames, never time.

It draws into KMS dumb buffers (a legacy SETCRTC on every connected
connector), or /dev/fb0 when there is no DRM device. No compositor, no
libdrm: the ioctls are called directly. With two buffers each frame is a page
flip on the vertical blank (the next frame starts when the flip event
arrives); where flips do not work (simpledrm) the region alone is copied
into the buffer on screen, paced at 60 Hz.

  flsplash             started by rc.sysinit as root; forks, exits 0 always
  flsplash --handover  asks the splash to end: it plays the animation to its end,
                       fades out (300 ms), drops DRM master and returns (waits up
                       to 12 s); flgreet and the first-boot step of rc.sysinit run
                       it before flcomp. flsplash exits 6 s after the release.
  flsplash --text      the boot text now (also puts the console back to text;
                       sends HUP)
  flsplash --render MS W H FILE.ppm
                       the picture at MS ms on a W x H screen, written to a
                       PPM; needs no screen (how to look at it while tuning
                       genart.c)

Signals: USR1 is the handover: the splash lets go when its animation is over
(flat: it fades to the background first, so the login screen starts from that
colour). HUP or TERM show the text at once; `flsplash --text` sends HUP. USR2,
which a failed rc `step` sends, is only logged: the splash goes on (round 20: a
boot step never ends or changes the splash). Esc shows the text at once (tty0 is
read in raw mode). The animation clock runs only while a screen is up, so a card
that is gone for a moment does not use the animation up. Kernel option
`nosplash` turns it off. With KD_GRAPHICS set on tty0, the status lines of rc go
to the console buffer only (the serial console still gets them).

When the DRM device goes away (simpledrm giving way to the real driver) the
card is opened again and drawing continues at the right point: /dev and
/dev/dri are watched with inotify, vanished cards answer ENODEV, and mappings
of an unplugged card raise SIGBUS, which is caught. If no screen shows up
within 4 s it exits and the boot stays text. Nobody taking over within 3
minutes also means text.

Files in /run: flsplash.pid, .done (animation over), .released (handed over),
.style (flat or legacy: the login screen reads it, see flgreet's README),
.log (what happened, in ms).

flsplash-set (src/flsplash/flsplash-set.c): the Themes app's helper, installed
setuid root, group seat (mode 4750, recipe owner= line). `flsplash-set flat|legacy`
writes /etc/freelfs/splash.conf after the person's password (flauth, the same way
flpkg-seat does). It checks its single argument, that the caller passes flauth
(or is root), that /etc/freelfs is a root-owned directory not writable by others
(created 0755 when missing), and it writes a new file with O_EXCL and no symlink
followed, then renames it over splash.conf. Anything else at that place is refused.

Tests: `make check` (tests/check.sh): both styles render, the config picks the
style, the helper's arguments, the write and the refusals. No screen, no root.

Planned, not built: custom boot screens. A user image (a PNG or the logo colour
scheme) with the same spinner, chosen in Themes > Boot screen and kept in
/etc/freelfs/ next to splash.conf; the flat style is the template for it. Until
then the two styles above are the only choices.

Build: make (genart needs FreeType and the IBM Plex Sans font, flsplash needs
zlib); make install (PREFIX, DESTDIR). The art file is generated, not kept in
the tree. The helper is built with it; the recipe gives it its owner and mode.

Wallpaper colours: `genart -W -a RRGGBB -w FILE` renders only the wallpaper (the 3D F without the
word) with the hue of the green material replaced (HSV: saturation and brightness stay, white
highlights stay white). The Makefile makes wallpaper-teal/blue/amber/orange/rose.flw this way next to
the green wallpaper.flw (same ids and colours as libflui's accents); Settings > Display > Wallpaper
offers them and flcomp loads the chosen one. The boot logo itself stays green.
